"stremler" <stremler@rohan.sdsu.edu> wrote:

> I like!

Well, I don't think I do. Why is having only the
guaranteed minimum set of altars in a game any less
just an additional minor challenge than inding no
shops except in Mines Town, or ... or than finding a
wand of wishing on level one is a frighteningly
unbalancing game advantage (which some of us aren't
competent to put to sufficient use to ascend anyway,
speaking from a couple of personal experiences)?

One thing NetHack deliberately fails to guarantee
is that each game be "fair" about the resources it
makes available to the player. Working around a
resource scarcity is a part of learning how to be a
skillful player. Just installing loopholes to make
them go away, it seems to me, dilutes the challenge
of "most consecutive ascensions" or "most ascensions
in 13 consecutive games" or whatever, and makes it
much harder to design tournament challenges that are
truly _challenging_ to the highly skilled player.

Getting good at winning nethack at all ever is
already a huge challenge. Having the game give you
only paltry "stuff", and being able to win despite
that,  is a higher level of challenge, thus Wade's
recent "but I can't catch you, this is already my
third caveman", a way to tell apart players at the
very top levels of play. Some of that ability to do
fine discriminations should remain in the vanilla
game, or all we have left is conducts.

IMO

